body:
## Summary

`VideoFrame::fromAVFrame` (`src/video/videoframe.cpp:195-212`) has a time-of-check-time-of-use race condition during read-to-write lock upgrade on the static `refsLock`.

## Details

```cpp
refsLock.lockForRead();
if (!refsMap.contains(sourceID)) {
    refsLock.unlock();         // <-- gap: lock is released
    refsLock.lockForWrite();   // <-- another thread can slip in here
    // No re-check of refsMap.contains(sourceID)!
}
const QMutexLocker<QMutex> sourceMutexLock{&mutexMap[sourceID]};
```

Two threads can simultaneously:
1. Both see `sourceID` not in `refsMap` under read lock
2. Both release read lock
3. Both acquire write lock sequentially
4. Both attempt to insert into `mutexMap` via `operator[]`

The `mutexMap[sourceID]` call using `operator[]` can insert into a `std::unordered_map` while other threads are reading it (those that passed the `contains` check), causing iterator invalidation and undefined behavior.

## Suggested Fix

After upgrading to write lock, re-check the condition:

```cpp
refsLock.lockForRead();
if (!refsMap.contains(sourceID)) {
    refsLock.unlock();
    refsLock.lockForWrite();
    // Re-check after lock upgrade
    if (!refsMap.contains(sourceID)) {
        // Initialize the entry
    }
}
```

Or always acquire write lock upfront (simpler but less concurrent).

## Impact

Potential crash or memory corruption when multiple video sources start simultaneously (e.g., joining a group video call).
